/**@page dev/goals Design goals

To facilitate remote data acquisition, which may happen on a headless system,
recording and displaying data are separated into a backend (the recording 
service or daemon, respectively) and the frontend (the user interface). The 
following diagram gives an idea of the components and the acquisition data flow: 

@image html overview.svg



@subsection dev-backend Backend

The backend must—with high reliability and speed—record video frames and 
additional data from various sources. It should be implemented in the sense
of a system service (which also means, it should automatically start on system
boot). The service must not crash on partial failures (such as device errors) 
but continue to operate.

The software must avoid blocking, for example caused by the hardware. In case
of failures, data should be recoverable (bad example: the DevWareX software, while
recording a camera in triggered mode, cannot be stopped when the camera does
not receive any trigger any more. Then, the only option is to kill the software.
In this case, additional information is not written to the disk, so that, for
example MP4 files, are corrupted).


@subsubsection dev-backend-monitoring Monitoring

Monitoring refers to several aspects. One major aspect is hard-disk space and
health which must be reported to the user interface regularly. For a remote 
system there are more things to consider, for example temperature or battery 
status, etc.

<b>Requires</b>:<br/>
@ref core-logging<br/>
@ref core-sysinfo<br/>


@subsubsection dev-backend-devices Devices

The major devices, that must be respresented initially are Basler USB3Vision 
products (via [pylon Software](https://www.baslerweb.com/de-de/software/pylon/)) 
and OnSemi/Aptina Demo3 (via [DevWareX](https://aptina.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DEVS/pages/3244099/DevWareX)).
Additionally, it appears favorable, also to support standard UVC cameras.

Future development, might however, also require additional data, such as GNSS
coordinates.

@TBD{The first plugin to develop is a dummy video device which should serve
as basis for benchmarking.}

<b>Requires</b>:<br/>
@ref core-plugins<br/>
@ref core-storage<br/>
@ref core-settings<br/>


@subsubsection dev-backend-storage Storage

Storage, here, refers both to how data is stored on the hard-disk as well as 
how data is provisioned for transmission as well as real-time analysis. 
Additionally, plugins may provide video compression or data formats (such as
HDF5). 

@TBD{Encoding (lossy or lossless) may happen after data is recorded
in a raw format.}

<b>Requires</b>:<br/>
@ref core-storage<br/>
@ref core-plugins<br/>


@subsubsection dev-backend-realtime Real-time interface

@TBD{The "real-time interface" will probably only require storage access. 
Implementation-wise we need to think if such access to the data must be granted
also to external programs.}


@subsubsection dev-backend-transport Transport

Data transmission from the backend to the frontend (over network or locally)
must provide different channels. The "high priority" channel used for recording
and possibly real-time analysis is part of @ref core-storage. Second, a 
"control channel" is needed, that does not allow packet loss. Third, a
"data channel" must be provided, which can be lossy (i.e. while no recorded 
image must be lost when writing to disk, not every image has to be displayed
in the user-interface).

<b>Requires</b>:<br/>
@ref core-transport<br/>


@subsubsection dev-backend-settings Settings

"Settings" encompasses how program-wide settings are stored in a configuration
file as well as how settings are applied, for example to plugins.

<b>Requires</b>:<br/>
@ref core-settings<br/>


@subsection dev-frontend Frontend

@TBD{
    A good starting point for designing a user interface is Basler's 
    [pylon Viewer](https://docs.baslerweb.com/overview-of-the-pylon-viewer).
    
    What has to be kept in mind:
    - display of multiple cameras
    - triggered, non-trigger mode (→ "arm" and "start" button)
    - image enhancement
    - overlays (such as the cross-hair above)
    - adjustment aids (such as contrast display)
    - maybe overlays (such as histograms)
    - multi-objective cameras (→ splitting)
    - rotation/mirroring
    - display of other data streams (e.g. audio)
    etc.
}




@section dev-milestone Milestones

@subsection milestone-0 Zero

The first implementation should implement @ref core-storage, specifically 
named shared memory and a semaphore with @ref core-plugins with a Basler® camera
plus a simple demo GUI displaying the images also using shared memory and the
semaphore.



@section dev-code Coding


@subsection dev-lang Language(s) and configuration

Core functionality should be provided in C++ (at least [C++20](https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/20)). 
Note, however, that the software must be compilable on at least Linux® and 
Windows®. For user-interface applications [Qt6](https://www.qt.io/product/framework) 
forms the basis. 


While the backend (the recording service or daemon, respectively) will rely
on compiled code, interfaces to other languages (mainly Python, but also
Matlab and more) will be provided. 
The frontend (user interface), though, could be a mixture of Python and C++ 
(see [Qt for Python](https://doc.qt.io/qtforpython-6/)). @TBD{With respect to
the backend, it should be discussed if a terminal UI should be provided
to simplify configuration or supervision, especially on a headless, remote
machine.}

For project configuration we use [CMake](https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/).


@subsection dev-git Version control

The "main" branch remains in the hands of BBO authors. External developers may 
not without explicit permission commit there. Instead, they must either use an 
arranged (read "agreed upon") branch or use github's pull-request feature.

@TBD{make user-interface independent (see vidre-gui on github)?}


@subsection dev-docs Documentation

API documentation is generated using [Doxygen](https://doxygen.nl/manual/index.html)
and published regularly on https://bbo-lab.github.io/vidre. The  
@ref api-core-util-uuid documentation should serve as a reference for how to 
document source code.

Documentation is, however, not limited to the source code. @TBD{Run-time 
documentation and help files are just as well required.}

Document as much as necessary, but not more (in case of doubt, follow
[XKCD 1343](https://xkcd.com/1343/) but not [XKCD 1481](https://xkcd.com/1481/)).



@section dev-impl Implementation


@subsection dev-core Core library


@subsubsection core-exception Error handling

Error handling, depending on context, uses either standard error codes
(e.g. [''std::errc''](https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/error/errc)) or 
exceptions. See @ref api-core-exception for further information.


@subsubsection core-logging Logging

@TBD{Logging must differentiate between priority messages, which must be
transmitted to the user-interface and rather local logging, which may include
debug information. The format should be decided after message passing has
implemented.}


@subsubsection core-format Data format

For in-memory data, both acquisition (e.g. video frames) and meta-data (e.g. 
video format and dimensions), as well as data transmission, we suggest 
[TLV](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Type%E2%80%93length%E2%80%93value) encoding
(see also [DER](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X.690#DER_encoding)). Note, 
however, that endianess must be taken into account (and be present in all 
files, akin to the byte-order-mark in Unicode).

@TBD{A video frame, for example, could be implemented in the following way 
(indentation is used for the values):

    <T:META><L><V:
        <T:FRAME_ID><L><V:frame index>
        <T:FRAME_TS><L><V:frame timestamp>
        ... the initial meta data must also contain format, encoding, etc. ...
    >
    <T:FRAME><L><V:DATA>

This can, of course, be extended to any data format, e.g. chunks of audio.}



@subsubsection core-storage Storage

Storage, here, encompasses both, memory and disk storage.

@par "Memory"
For reasons of speed and to facilitate options for real-time analysis, memory 
should be implemented as a ring buffer using shared memory. The shared memory
should be named (thus it can be shared also to other processes and not only
loadable modules). 

Each data-generating device (plugin) reserves it's own ring buffer and solely 
writes to it. A named semaphore can then be used to inform other threads and
processes to inform about the new data chunk.


@par "Disk"
Disk storage should be provided through plugins. The initial implementation
will only require raw storage, future options may use lossy or lossles 
compression, or, even only store a video frame partially.

@TBD{Alternatively, data could also be transformed post-hoc in formats,
such as HDF5.}

@TBD{
    Storing frames partially, for example selected using object tracking, 
    would raise the question of filtering prior to raw data storage. The API
    should at least provide a way to create multiple data streams that can
    be configured apropriately. Then the "disk writer" process or thread
    must be informed no to use the "high priority" shared memory from the
    device but from the filter.
}

@subsubsection core-plugins Plugins

Plugins may come as loadable modules or external programs. 
@TBD{They are organized hierarchically:

    device
        video
            pylon
            onsemi
    storage
        raw
    ...

}

Each plugin is accompanied by a configuration file, together with documentation.
@TBD{It must be distinguished between information about the plugin itself,
as well as configuration options that can be passed to it. It seems sensible
to also encode user-interface layout for a "settings page". See e.g.
USB3Vision, GenICam, etc.}. See also @ref core-settings below.

@subsubsection core-sysinfo System information

@TBD{The format of how to provide system information, such as harddisk space
and health, as well as other information, such as CPU temperature or the like,
is complex. This information has to be passed between back- and frontend 
independent of the mode of communication.}

@see @ref core-transport


@subsubsection core-transport Transport

As mentioned above, there are three modes of data transmission. The 
high-priority, or raw data, respectively stream is covered by the storage/memory
handler. Therefore, two more tranmission modes, that also need to be 
"networkable" need to be integrated. Note that networking is a feature that
will be supplemented in the longer run, the API only needs to be prepared for
such an extension.

@par "Control"
The control channel should be a named pipe for local transmission and TCP
for a networked connection. 

@par "Data"
The local data channel my use the shared ring buffer (and the semaphore) 
describe above (see @ref core-storage) directly. For a network connection
UDP is sufficient.

@see @ref core-format

@subsubsection core-settings Settings

As core configuration and settings format, VidRe will use Lua. The advantage of
Lua is, that configuration files for cameras, where settings may require to
adjust multiple camera registers, may implement wrapper functions.

For easy use, the following should be implemented:
```.cpp
class options {
    class entry; // basically a variant, that can hold maps, arrays and values

    options(const std::string &json);
    options(const std::initializer_list<entry> &table);
};
```
which would allow to write
```.cpp
plugin["device/video/uvc"].setup({ 
    { "fps", 20 }, 
    { "geometry", {
        {"x", 0}, 
        {"y", 0}, 
        {"width", 320},
        {"height", 200}
    }}
});
```
instead of 
```.cpp
plugin["device/video/uvc"].setup("{ \"fps\": 20, … }");
```

*/